{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'risk-management' defense fails because it conflates the logical status of a theory (high content + survived) with a pragmatic rule of action that implicitly relies on inductive expectations. Popper explicitly rejected using corroboration as a guide for action if it implies any expectation of future success; he argued we choose the best-tested theory only to subject it to further tests, not because it is 'least error-prone' or offers 'greatest potential' in a predictive sense. By framing the preference as avoiding error or maximizing learning potential, the target smuggles in an inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past (i.e., that a theory that survived severe tests is less likely to fail tomorrow), which violates Popper's strict prohibition against inferring future reliability from past performance.","premise":"Popper held that corroboration reports only past survival and cannot justify any expectation of future reliability or reduced error probability.","premises_essential":["87ba80dbf11d3a155eb3832897f5bd5013843595e3590617d90aa689889963ab"],"successor_question":"If Popper forbids using corroboration to predict future success, how can he consistently recommend acting on the best-corroborated theory without contradicting his own rejection of induction?","target":"87ba80dbf11d3a155eb3832897f5bd5013843595e3590617d90aa689889963ab"}]}